      "title": "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "cf0738bf-7c00-57d3-b243-d7667977e3a7",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={6}^{7x-5}$ at the point where $x=1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={6}^{u},\\,u=7x-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={6}^{u}\\ln(6),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={6}^{u}\\ln(6)\\cdot (7) \\\\ & =(7)\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =7\\cdot {6}^{7x-5}\\ln(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=1} & =(7)\\cdot {6}^{7(1)-5}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =7\\cdot {6}^{2}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =252 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is $252 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=1$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={6}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(36)&=(252 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)})\\left(x-(1)\\right) \\\\ y&=252 x \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)} - 252 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)} + 36.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
